course: dictionary
lesson: 15328
title: Snips: cuts, pieces and bargains
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: The word snips can be a verb, a noun or a tool, and the sentence tells you which. When someone snips something, they cut it with small, quick cuts of scissors. «Mia snips the paper into stars.» Mia is doing the cutting, so snips is a verb here.
explain: Snips can also be a noun, meaning small quick cuts or the small pieces cut off. «The floor was covered in paper snips after the craft lesson.» Those are the little bits that were cut off, and the words around them show it.
explain: Tin snips are a tool, not a pair of school scissors. «Dad used tin snips to cut the metal sheet in the workshop.» Only use them with an adult.
explain: In informal British speech, «a snip» means a real bargain. «The coat was £5. It's a snip!» The price tells you it is very cheap for what you get.
fact: Snips can be a verb: he snips, she snips, they snip.
fact: «Snips» means small, quick cuts made with scissors, or the small pieces that are cut off.
fact: Tin snips are strong shears for cutting sheet metal, and they are used in workshops. Use them only with an adult.
fact: In UK informal speech, «a snip» means a real bargain, something cheap for its value.
fact: «Snips and snails» comes from an old rhyme about little boys.
exercise: «The hairdresser snips the ends of my hair.» What does snips mean here? | Wraps something up in paper | Sings a happy song | *Cuts with small, quick cuts | Sticks two things together
exercise: «It's a snip at £5!» What does snip mean here? | *A real bargain, very cheap | A loud noise | A broken toy | A small cut in the paper
exercise: «He tidied up the paper snips from the floor.» What are the snips? | *Small pieces cut off the paper | Pieces of cake | Big cardboard boxes | Friends from school
test: Tin snips are used for what? | Cutting bread for lunch | *Cutting sheet metal in a workshop | Cutting hair at the salon | Cutting paper for art class
test: «Snips and snails» comes from what? | *An old rhyme about little boys | A recipe for dinner | A football match | A weather forecast
exam: «Sam snips the string, and the box springs open.» What does snips mean in this sentence? | Ties something tightly | *Cuts with small, quick cuts | Small pieces of tin | A real bargain
